Here is the full test smell audit of `CatalogTest.java` against the testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 2           | 2              |
| **Total**| **10**      | **10**         |

Clean tests: **1** (`addThrowsOnNegativeCount`)

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic — `addConditionalLogic()`

**Lines 26–36.** The `if (sku.equals("b"))` branch inside the loop means a path selection is hidden inside the test. If the branching logic is wrong, certain items get the wrong quantity and the composite assertion at the end may still accidentally pass.

```java
// smell: branching changes what is exercised
if (sku.equals("b")) {
    catalog.add(sku, 10);
} else {
    catalog.add(sku, 1);
}
assertEquals(12, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
```

**Refactor → explicit, linear setup with per-item assertions:**

```java
@Test
void addAccumulatesPerSku() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 10);
    catalog.add("c", 1);

    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(10, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** A branch may silently never execute; the summed assertion masks per-SKU failures (also an Assertion Roulette concern).

---

#### 2. Mystery Guest — `addLoadsFromFile()`

**Lines 39–46.** The test creates a temp file, writes to it, reads it back, parses it, and feeds it to the catalog. The file system is an external resource invisible to someone reading the test, and the file I/O can fail for reasons unrelated to `Catalog`.

```java
Path tmp = Files.createTempFile("catalog", ".txt");
Files.writeString(tmp, "x:7");
String[] parts = Files.readString(tmp).split(":");
catalog.add(parts[0], Integer.parseInt(parts[1]));
assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
```

**Refactor → inline the data, remove the file system dependency:**

```java
@Test
void addMakesItemInStock() {
    catalog.add("x", 7);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures from file-system permissions, temp-dir quotas, or CI sandboxing. The file is also never cleaned up.

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test — `addSleepy()`

**Lines 49–54.** `Thread.sleep(200)` adds 200 ms of dead wait to a purely synchronous, in-memory operation. There is nothing asynchronous to wait for.

```java
catalog.add("a", 1);
Thread.sleep(200);
assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
```

**Refactor → remove the sleep entirely:**

```java
@Test
void addSingleItem() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Multiplied across a large suite, gratuitous sleeps add minutes of wasted CI time and can cause flaky timeout failures.

---

#### 4. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — `addAndDoNothing()`

**Lines 57–60.** The test calls `catalog.add("a", 1)` and returns. No assertion, no expected exception. The test passes even if `add()` silently corrupts state.

```java
@Test
void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
}
```

**Refactor → assert the observable side effect:**

```java
@Test
void addStoresQuantity() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test inflates the pass count and coverage metrics without verifying any behavior.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM

#### 5. Eager Test — `doEverythingAtOnce()`

**Lines 63–73.** This single test calls **4 distinct production methods** (`add`, `quantity`, `inStock`, `toString`) yet only asserts on the summed quantity. The `inStock` and `toString` calls are exercised but never verified.

```java
catalog.add("a", 1);  catalog.add("b", 2);  catalog.add("c", 3);
catalog.quantity("a"); catalog.quantity("b");
catalog.inStock("c");
catalog.toString();
assertEquals(6, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
```

**Refactor → split into focused tests, one behavior each:**

