#### 7. Sensitive Equality — `toStringFormat()`

**Lines 84–88.** The assertion is hard-coupled to the exact `toString()` format string `"Catalog(items=1)"`. Any cosmetic change to the format (e.g., adding a space, changing the field name) breaks the test even though `Catalog` behavior is unchanged.

```java
assertEquals("Catalog(items=1)", catalog.toString());
```

**Refactor → assert on the semantic properties instead:**

```java
@Test
void toStringContainsItemCount() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertTrue(catalog.toString().contains("1"));
}
```

Or, if the exact format is a public contract, document that explicitly in the test name (e.g., `toStringFormatIsStable`) so future maintainers know the coupling is intentional.

**Risk if unfixed:** Brittle test that breaks on harmless formatting changes.

